#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of 2x2 Systems of Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115476 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d07d2d5c-d3e0-595b-84ff-e18597a3d040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1061 16471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "836cc4ee-5cd5-58a8-8fc6-487e670dc062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116110 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1061 16472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "33db29bf-42ca-5174-b04b-c5529dc2bc98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116111 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1061 16473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a71d8e7-c420-59a3-927f-619b1fbcc37e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116112 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1061 16474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "72cfa9ea-e123-5f2c-bad3-38a7904ca9f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider a system of two linear equations with two variables \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand let\n![](images/73/73c4f1122f53beb4bea2619922226e641d7ac1dd2bb611106d8a33dc13a7abbd.svg)\n\nRecall that we can write the system as $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{b}.$ The **invertible matrix theorem** says the following statements are equivalent:\n\n- $A$ is invertible (that is, $A^{-1}$ exists).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ has exactly one solution for any $2 \\times 1$ column vector $\\mathbf{b}$ (we call such systems consistent independent).\n\n- The system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ has the unique solution ![](images/9f/9f196772fa8ca5829a00c8f4378f2a51f423cf6e27fdc8470e245be0c4005f2c.svg)\n\n- The lines ${\\color{red}{a_{11}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{12}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_1}}$ and ${\\color{red}{a_{21}}}x+{\\color{red}{a_{22}}}y={\\color{blue}{b_2}}$ intersect at one point.\n\n- The  determinant of $A$ is nonzero (that is, $\\det(A) \\neq 0$)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1065 3050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5a0ce34c-f39a-5c24-bab3-840ff974402c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116491 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451237 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451238 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451239 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has the unique  solution $⟨0,0⟩$\n- **III.** $⟨5,-4⟩$ is a solution to $Ax=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b252a44d-8417-5bdc-8a9b-ad6e81276b7c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323750 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-6\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. Again,  according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452649 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452650 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452651 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73567" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne 3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** The lines $2x+2y=2$ and $3x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "946ddb30-e9a3-52e2-ad46-5aa2c1f15eef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186342017 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $2k-6\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne 3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- Statement III is true by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statement III is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454723 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454724 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454725 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1071 "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A$ is **not** invertible\n- **II.** The lines $2x-3y=1$ and $2x-2y=2$ intersect at exactly one point\n- **III.** $Ax=0$ has no solution" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aafb0544-5c12-5527-bc2a-2417215aad99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377668 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. \n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false since, according to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473685 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473686 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473687 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1069 "q-128139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If a $2\\times 2$-matrix $A$ is invertible, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n\nI. $det(A)\\ne 0$\nII. $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution\nIII. $Ax=0$ has infinitely many solutions\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2d9404d-2d42-56c6-b91b-5351ccec57cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186400331 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is invertible, $\\operatorname{det}(A)\\ne 0.$ Also, for each vector $b,$ the equation $Ax=b$ has the unique solution $x=A^{-1}b.$ In particular, $Ax=0$ has only the zero solution. Thus statements I and II are true and statement III is false. The correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186450566 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450566 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450566 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f00f19b1-a5d9-5f3f-892f-2e3d8e66d32c" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450567 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450567 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450567 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f24b70da-3d6e-5768-987e-5a6a1111b77e" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450568 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450568 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450568 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40c3344c-65d7-58be-ae26-42bb9b9f586a" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450569 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450569 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450569 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "740835e0-32e5-50d4-b9e7-dfc4013e2ecf" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450570 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450570 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450570 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f9e3557-049b-5e3a-bce9-8906dde445a5" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450571 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450571 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186450571 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9efcb6d2-5f9b-556f-ba0b-a54c998fb8e2" #edb/ref 175