592186494886 #edb/ref 1080 "II  only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494886 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494886 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "efaad133-59c6-5b2b-9d22-976c341550bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186494886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186494448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186494934 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "273b6a9c-3dfc-5014-9117-506724cb00ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495058 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495058 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495058 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495058 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines $y-x=1$ and $x+ky=4$, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\nI. the lines $y-x=0$ and $x+ky=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. the lines $y-x=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. $det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495058 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "11aeb2f0-20f1-51d5-8c52-39a1558cd757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495058 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNotice that if we set \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  \n\nthen the fourth part of the theorem is true. Hence,  all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III give us a true statement: \n\n- *If the lines $y-x=1$ and $x+ky=4$, where $k$ is a constant, intersect at exactly one point, then $det[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495176 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495203 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495176 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "028bab25-3fed-5252-aa96-72f7de3058fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495176 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186323750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495203 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495203 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "59b4b15c-df1d-52b3-a1d2-1425a0066736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495203 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495220 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "882122b9-54f2-5353-858e-8a741a0617ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495220 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186400331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495224 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495224 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ca0b9cc-1fcf-55ba-84e5-387e60cec625" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495224 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186377668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495228 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495220 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495228 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96da7574-bc62-58b8-af70-6d04a234aff0" #